saveOutputs: false
cells:
  - kind: 1
    value: "# 11e · Step 4 – Which part of the knee is injured? (RDF knowledge graph)\n\nWe have a suspicion, but no answer: the keyword points to the cruciate ligament, the similar texts point to a sprain. Similar is not the same. The knee knowledge graph knows anatomy, findings, injury mechanisms and injury patterns – as rules a machine can check and explain.\n\n**Question:** Do Jonas' findings fit the pattern of a sprain or of a cruciate ligament rupture – and why?\n\n- **Features:** RDF Graph: `SEM_MATCH` (SPARQL in SQL), rulebase and entailment, graph collection with the Foundational Model of Anatomy (FMA), join of RDF and relational data\n- **Pattern:** M1 – find, then check (part 2: check)\n- **Data:** RDF network `RDF_NETWORK` of the user DOAG2026: graph `KNEE_KG` (knee knowledge graph), graph `FMA`, entailment `KNEE_KG_RIX` (OWLPRIME + rulebase `KNEE_RB`), graph collection `KNEE_FMA_VM`; relational tables `DIAGNOSES` and `MEDICATION_RECOMMENDATIONS` (sections 7 and 14)\n- **Run:** as the demo user (DOAG2026) in SQL Developer for VS Code, after `08_create_rdf_network.sql`, `09a_load_knee_kg.sql` and `09b_load_fma.sql`. Run the cells from top to bottom.\n\n`SEM_MATCH` needs Oracle Java in the Autonomous AI Database. Network owner (`DOAG2026`) and network name (`RDF_NETWORK`) are the last two arguments of every call; adjust them if your demo user has another name. Prefix `kg:` = `https://doag2026.example/knee#`; Jonas' report is the resource `kg:Report_SR-2026-11-16-0001`.\n\n> Fictitious demo: no medical device and no medical advice. Places from OpenStreetMap (© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL); all persons and story places are synthetic."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Session settings\n\nThe story takes place on Monday, 16 November 2026, in Franconia. With the session time zone `Europe/Berlin`, all timestamps and literals read in local time.\n\nThe SQLcl settings in the second cell make the text output readable: columns as wide as their content instead of their declared length (`SQLFORMAT ansiconsole`; values from JSON are `VARCHAR2(4000)`), one header per result (`PAGESIZE`), wider lines (`LINESIZE`) and complete JSON documents and CLOBs (`LONG`, `LONGCHUNKSIZE`)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "ALTER SESSION SET TIME_ZONE = 'Europe/Berlin';\r\n"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "SET SQLFORMAT ansiconsole\nSET PAGESIZE 1000\nSET LINESIZE 250\nSET LONG 1000000\nSET LONGCHUNKSIZE 250\n"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 1. What is in the graphs?\n\nThe knowledge comes from two graphs: our own knee graph and the Foundational Model of Anatomy, the reference ontology of human anatomy.\n\n**Question:** What do the two graphs contain?\n\n**How the query answers it:** Each RDF graph has a view `RDF_NETWORK#RDFT_<GRAPH>` with one row per triple (subject, predicate, object).\n\n- `KNEE_KG`: anatomy of the knee, findings, injury mechanisms, injury patterns as rules, synonyms – and Jonas' report as resource.\n- `FMA`: the Foundational Model of Anatomy, the reference ontology for human anatomy (CC BY 3.0).\n\n**Result:** 353 triples in `KNEE_KG` and 612,982 in `FMA` – the complete reference ontology, loaded as it is published. The knee graph is small and written for this case; through `skos:exactMatch` its 13 anatomical classes point into the large FMA."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT 'KNEE_KG' AS rdf_graph, COUNT(*) AS triples FROM rdf_network#rdft_knee_kg\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'FMA',                  COUNT(*)            FROM rdf_network#rdft_fma;\n"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 2. Jonas' report in the knowledge graph\n\nJonas' app report is also in the graph, with the findings and the mechanism taken from his answers.\n\n**Question:** Which findings and which mechanism does the graph know for Jonas?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The app report is also a resource in the graph: findings (`kg:reportsFinding`), mechanism (`kg:reportsMechanism`), affected structure, side and patient. `SEM_MATCH` runs a SPARQL query and returns the variables as columns; `OPTIONAL` adds the German label where there is one.\n\n**Result:** Jonas' report as data: a twist (*Verdrehtrauma*) of the right knee joint with four findings – audible \"plopp\", instability, pain when walking and rapid swelling (under 2 hours; the knee swelled within about 10 minutes). Report ID and patient link it to the JSON document from 11a. What the searches in 11c had to guess from words is stated here explicitly: *Plopp* is `kg:AudiblePop`, *wackelig* is `kg:GivingWay`."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT predicate, obj AS object_value, label\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n          PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n          SELECT ?predicate ?obj ?label\n          WHERE {\n            <https://doag2026.example/knee#Report_SR-2026-11-16-0001> ?predicate ?obj .\n            OPTIONAL { ?obj rdfs:label ?label . FILTER (lang(?label) = \"de\") }\n          }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK'))\nORDER  BY predicate, obj;\n"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 3. Check the injury patterns\n\nEach injury pattern says which findings it needs, how many of them, and which findings argue against it.\n\n**Question:** Which pattern do Jonas' findings meet – a sprain or a cruciate ligament rupture?\n\n**How the query answers it:** Each injury pattern names its strong indicators (`kg:hasStrongIndicator`), the number it needs (`kg:minStrongIndicators`) and findings that argue against it (`kg:argumentAgainst`). The SPARQL query counts per pattern how many of Jonas' findings are strong indicators and how many argue against it; SQL then decides.\n\n**Result:**\n- *Muster vordere Kreuzbandruptur* (ACL rupture): 3 of 3 strong indicators – Plopp, rapid swelling, giving way – 2 needed, nothing against it. **Fits.**\n- *Muster Kniedistorsion* (sprain): only one of the two indicators it needs (pain when walking), and three findings argue against it. **Does not fit.**\n- The other three patterns find none of their strong indicators in Jonas' report. Meniscus lesion would at least match the twist, but has no indicator; the contusion has a finding against it.\n\nThe vote in 11c said sprain. The rules say: no pattern has more findings against it than the sprain – three.\n\n*\"Similar is not the same. Not every swollen knee is a sprain.\"*"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT pattern_label,\n       TO_NUMBER(strong_found)   AS strong_found,\n       TO_NUMBER(strong_needed)  AS strong_needed,\n       TO_NUMBER(counter_found)  AS counter_found,\n       TO_NUMBER(mechanism_ok)   AS mechanism_matches,\n       CASE WHEN TO_NUMBER(strong_found) >= TO_NUMBER(strong_needed)\n             AND TO_NUMBER(counter_found) = 0\n             AND TO_NUMBER(mechanism_ok)  > 0\n            THEN 'fits' ELSE 'does not fit' END AS verdict\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n          PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n          SELECT ?pattern_label ?strong_needed\n                 (COUNT(DISTINCT ?strong)  AS ?strong_found)\n                 (COUNT(DISTINCT ?counter) AS ?counter_found)\n                 (COUNT(DISTINCT ?mech)    AS ?mechanism_ok)\n          WHERE {\n            ?pattern a kg:InjuryPattern ;\n                     kg:minStrongIndicators ?strong_needed ;\n                     rdfs:label ?pattern_label .\n            FILTER (lang(?pattern_label) = \"de\")\n            OPTIONAL { <https://doag2026.example/knee#Report_SR-2026-11-16-0001> kg:reportsFinding ?strong .\n                       ?pattern kg:hasStrongIndicator ?strong }\n            OPTIONAL { <https://doag2026.example/knee#Report_SR-2026-11-16-0001> kg:reportsFinding ?counter .\n                       ?pattern kg:argumentAgainst ?counter }\n            OPTIONAL { <https://doag2026.example/knee#Report_SR-2026-11-16-0001> kg:reportsMechanism ?mech .\n                       ?pattern kg:requiresMechanism ?mech }\n          }\n          GROUP BY ?pattern_label ?strong_needed',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK'))\nORDER  BY TO_NUMBER(strong_found) DESC, TO_NUMBER(counter_found), pattern_label;\n"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 4. The same knowledge as rules: inferred triples\n\nThe same check can be written once as rules and computed in advance by the reasoner.\n\n**Question:** What does the reasoner derive from Jonas' report?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The rulebase `KNEE_RB` holds three rules (see `08_create_rdf_network.sql`): a reported finding that is a strong indicator of a pattern creates `kg:showsIndicatorOf`, a counter-indicator creates `kg:showsCounterIndicatorOf`, a matching mechanism `kg:matchesMechanismOf`. The entailment `KNEE_KG_RIX` has derived these triples in advance. Passing `SEM_RULEBASES('OWLPRIME', 'KNEE_RB')` makes them visible to the query.\n\nAn RDF graph stores each triple only once: three strong indicators of the same pattern derive the same triple *report – `kg:showsIndicatorOf` – pattern*. The rules therefore say *whether* a relation holds, not how often; section 3 counts. The query shows one row per pattern with the derived relations.\n\n**The rules in plain language (slide 13):**\n\n| Source | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| `KNEE_RB` · `strong_indicator_rule` | **IF** the report mentions a finding **AND** it is a strong sign of a pattern **THEN** the report shows a sign of that pattern (`kg:showsIndicatorOf`) |\n| `KNEE_RB` · `counter_indicator_rule` | **IF** the report mentions a finding **AND** it speaks against a pattern **THEN** the report has a counter-sign (`kg:showsCounterIndicatorOf`) |\n| `KNEE_RB` · `mechanism_rule` | **IF** the report names how it happened **AND** the pattern needs that mechanism **THEN** the mechanism fits (`kg:matchesMechanismOf`) |\n| `OWLPRIME` | **IF** A is a kind of B **AND** B is a kind of C **THEN** A is a kind of C (`rdfs:subClassOf` is transitive, section 12) |\n| FMA, at query time | **IF** our structure is an FMA class (`skos:exactMatch`) **AND** the FMA says that class is part of the knee joint **THEN** the structure is part of the knee joint (sections 9, 13, 14) |\n\nExample from the slide: Jonas' report mentions *audible pop* (`kg:reportsFinding`), and *audible pop* is a strong sign of the ACL tear pattern (`kg:hasStrongIndicator`). Nobody stored \"the report shows a sign of the ACL tear pattern\" – the first rule concludes it.\n\n**Result:** the rules confirm section 3 without a single `COUNT` in SPARQL. The cruciate ligament rupture is the only pattern with an indicator, the matching mechanism and no counter-indicator. The sprain has all three relations – including the counter-indicator. The contusion only has a counter-indicator, the meniscus lesion only the mechanism. The medial ligament strain is missing: Jonas' report has no relation to it at all."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT pattern_label,\n       MAX(CASE WHEN relation = 'https://doag2026.example/knee#showsIndicatorOf'        THEN 'yes' END) AS shows_indicator,\n       MAX(CASE WHEN relation = 'https://doag2026.example/knee#showsCounterIndicatorOf' THEN 'yes' END) AS shows_counter_indicator,\n       MAX(CASE WHEN relation = 'https://doag2026.example/knee#matchesMechanismOf'      THEN 'yes' END) AS matches_mechanism\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n          PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n          SELECT ?relation ?pattern_label\n          WHERE {\n            <https://doag2026.example/knee#Report_SR-2026-11-16-0001> ?relation ?pattern .\n            ?pattern rdfs:label ?pattern_label .\n            FILTER (?relation IN (kg:showsIndicatorOf, kg:showsCounterIndicatorOf, kg:matchesMechanismOf))\n            FILTER (lang(?pattern_label) = \"de\")\n          }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), SEM_RULEBASES('OWLPRIME', 'KNEE_RB'), NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK'))\nGROUP  BY pattern_label\nORDER  BY pattern_label;\n"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 5. The explanation: from the pattern to the structure in the FMA\n\nA diagnosis without a reason is not trustworthy. The graph can follow the chain from the pattern to the anatomy and the next step.\n\n**Question:** Which structure is injured, what is it called in the reference anatomy, and what should happen next?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The graph collection `KNEE_FMA_VM` combines `KNEE_KG`, the `FMA` and the inferred triples, so one query can follow the chain:\n\npattern → suggests injury (`kg:suggests`) → affects structure (`kg:affects`) → same as FMA class (`skos:exactMatch`) → FMA label, plus recommendation (`kg:recommends`) and urgency (`kg:urgencyHours`).\n\n**Result:** ACL rupture → *anterior cruciate ligament* = FMA 44614 (*Anterior cruciate ligament*) → MRI within 48 hours."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT injury,\n       structure,\n       REGEXP_SUBSTR(fma_class, 'FMA_[0-9]+')  AS fma_id,\n       fma_label,\n       recommendation,\n       TO_NUMBER(urgency_hours)                AS urgency_hours\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n          PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n          SELECT ?injury ?structure ?fma_class ?fma_label ?recommendation ?urgency_hours\n          WHERE {\n            kg:Pattern_ACL kg:suggests     ?i ;\n                           kg:recommends   ?p ;\n                           kg:urgencyHours ?urgency_hours .\n            ?i rdfs:label ?injury .          FILTER (lang(?injury) = \"en\")\n            ?i kg:affects ?s .\n            ?s rdfs:label ?structure .       FILTER (lang(?structure) = \"en\")\n            ?p rdfs:label ?recommendation .  FILTER (lang(?recommendation) = \"en\")\n            OPTIONAL { ?s skos:exactMatch ?fma_class .\n                       OPTIONAL { ?fma_class rdfs:label ?fma_label } }\n          }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_FMA_VM'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK'));\n"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 6. The graph knows the synonyms\n\nIn step 3 someone had to type the synonyms of \"Plopp\" by hand.\n\n**Question:** Which words for an audible pop does the graph know?\n\n**How the query answers it:** In step 3 someone had to type `Knall OR geknallt OR Schnappen` by hand. The knowledge graph stores these words as `skos:altLabel` of the finding *AudiblePop*. `LISTAGG` turns them into a ready-made Oracle Text query; braces `{…}` protect multi-word phrases.\n\n**Result:** `{Knall} OR {Plopp} OR {Schnappen} OR {geknallt}` – exactly the three synonyms typed by hand in 11c, plus *Plopp* itself. Nobody has to know them any more; they are maintained once, in the graph, and every search can use them."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT LISTAGG('{' || alt_label || '}', ' OR ') WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY alt_label) AS contains_query\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n          PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n          SELECT ?alt_label\n          WHERE { kg:AudiblePop skos:altLabel ?alt_label }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK'));\n"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Use the synonyms from the graph in the full-text search\n\n**Question:** How many reports does the search find with the graph's vocabulary?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The PL/SQL block reads the synonyms from the graph and passes the generated query as a bind variable to `CONTAINS`. It counts the FHIR reports and the cruciate ligament ruptures among them. The output appears in the DBMS output of the cell.\n\n**Result:** 34 hits, 27 of them cruciate ligament ruptures. In 11c, \"Plopp\" alone found 12 reports with 7 ruptures; the synonyms typed by hand found 22 with 20. The query from the graph finds both sets – almost four times as many ruptures as the word Jonas used. The 7 other hits are mostly the negations again (*\"Kein Plopp\"*): the graph knows the words, but full text still cannot read *kein*. That is why step 4 judges Jonas' findings with the rules (section 3), not with a word search."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "DECLARE\n  l_query  VARCHAR2(4000);\n  l_hits   PLS_INTEGER;\n  l_acl    PLS_INTEGER;\nBEGIN\n  SELECT LISTAGG('{' || alt_label || '}', ' OR ') WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY alt_label)\n  INTO   l_query\n  FROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n          PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n          SELECT ?alt_label\n          WHERE { kg:AudiblePop skos:altLabel ?alt_label }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK'));\n\n  SELECT COUNT(*),\n         COUNT(CASE WHEN d.diagnosis_code = 'vkb_ruptur' THEN 1 END)\n  INTO   l_hits, l_acl\n  FROM   symptom_reports s\n  LEFT   JOIN diagnoses d ON d.report_id = s.report_id\n  WHERE  s.source = 'fhir'\n  AND    CONTAINS(s.report_text, l_query) > 0;\n\n  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Query from the graph: ' || l_query);\n  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Hits: ' || l_hits || ', of them cruciate ligament ruptures: ' || l_acl);\nEND;\n/\n"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 7. Knowledge meets case data: RDF and relational in one query\n\nKnowledge is only useful if it connects to real cases.\n\n**Question:** How often were the injuries the graph suggests actually diagnosed – and confirmed by MRI?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The injuries in the graph carry the diagnosis code of the case data (`kg:caseDiagnosisCode`, e.g. `vkb_ruptur`). So the result of `SEM_MATCH` can be joined with the relational table `DIAGNOSES` like any other row source.\n\n**Result:** the sprain is the most frequent knee diagnosis in the 1,000 records (115), ahead of the cruciate ligament rupture (80) and medial ligament strain and meniscus lesion (61 each). That frequency is why the nearest neighbours in 11c voted for the sprain. But all 80 ruptures and all 61 meniscus lesions were confirmed by MRI, none of the sprains: a sprain is a clinical diagnosis – a diagnosis of exclusion, as the rules in section 3 say. Frequent is not the same as likely for Jonas."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT k.pattern_label,\n       k.injury_label,\n       k.diagnosis_code,\n       COUNT(d.diagnosis_id)                                    AS diagnoses,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN d.confirmed_by = 'MRT' THEN 1 END)       AS confirmed_by_mri\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n          PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n          SELECT ?pattern_label ?injury_label ?diagnosis_code\n          WHERE {\n            ?pattern a kg:InjuryPattern ;\n                     rdfs:label  ?pattern_label ;\n                     kg:suggests ?injury .\n            ?injury rdfs:label ?injury_label ;\n                    kg:caseDiagnosisCode ?diagnosis_code .\n            FILTER (lang(?pattern_label) = \"de\")\n            FILTER (lang(?injury_label)  = \"en\")\n          }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK')) k\nLEFT   JOIN diagnoses d ON d.diagnosis_code = k.diagnosis_code\nGROUP  BY k.pattern_label, k.injury_label, k.diagnosis_code\nORDER  BY diagnoses DESC, k.pattern_label;\n"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 8. What the FMA adds (1): the classification of the anterior cruciate ligament\n\nThe knee graph names the structures. The Foundational Model of Anatomy (FMA) knows what they are.\n\n**Question:** Where does the anterior cruciate ligament sit in the reference anatomy?\n\n**How the query answers it:** In the FMA every class points to its parent with `rdfs:subClassOf`. The property path `rdfs:subClassOf*` walks up from FMA 44614 to the root in one pattern; a second path counts the levels above each class, so the rows come out in order. `FILTER (isIRI(...))` keeps the named classes – the blank nodes are OWL restrictions for the part-of relations (section 9).\n\n**Result:** twelve levels, from *Anterior cruciate ligament* via *Cruciate ligament*, *Ligament of knee joint*, *Skeletal ligament* and *Ligament organ* up to *Organ*, *Anatomical structure* and *Anatomical entity*.\n\n**What the FMA adds:** a curated classification of about 79,000 classes that nobody in the project has to model or maintain. The knee graph needs one link per structure (`skos:exactMatch`) and inherits all of it."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT fma_id,\n       label,\n       TO_NUMBER(depth) AS level_from_root\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX obo:      <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/>\n          PREFIX rdfs:     <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          PREFIX oboInOwl: <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#>\n          SELECT ?fma_id ?label (COUNT(?above) AS ?depth)\n          WHERE {\n            obo:FMA_44614 rdfs:subClassOf* ?class .\n            FILTER (isIRI(?class))\n            ?class rdfs:label  ?label ;\n                   oboInOwl:id ?fma_id .\n            ?class rdfs:subClassOf* ?above .\n            FILTER (isIRI(?above))\n          }\n          GROUP BY ?fma_id ?label',\n         SEM_MODELS('FMA'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK'))\nORDER  BY TO_NUMBER(depth) DESC;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 9. What the FMA adds (2): what else belongs to the knee joint?\n\n**Question:** Which structures does the FMA list as parts of the knee joint – and which of them does our knee graph know?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The OBO export of the FMA stores part-of relations as OWL restrictions: a class is a subclass of \"something that is `constitutional_part_of` the knee joint\" (`owl:Restriction` with `owl:onProperty` and `owl:someValuesFrom`). The query finds every class with such a restriction pointing to the knee joint (FMA 35175). On the graph collection `KNEE_FMA_VM` the `OPTIONAL` looks for the structure in `KNEE_KG` that maps to it.\n\n**Result:** 20 parts, all `constitutional_part_of`. 7 of them have a counterpart in the knee graph: anterior and posterior cruciate ligament, fibular collateral ligament, patellar ligament, medial and lateral meniscus, articular capsule. The others – for example the meniscofemoral ligament, the transverse ligament or the articular cartilage – are not covered by any injury pattern yet.\n\n**What the FMA adds:** a completeness check. The reference anatomy shows which parts of the knee the rules cover and which ones a future pattern could address."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT REGEXP_SUBSTR(relation, '[^#/]+$')             AS fma_relation,\n       part_label,\n       NVL(REGEXP_SUBSTR(kg_structure, '[^#]+$'), '–')   AS in_knee_kg\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX obo:  <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/>\n          PREFIX owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#>\n          PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n          SELECT ?relation ?part_label ?kg_structure\n          WHERE {\n            ?part rdfs:subClassOf ?restriction ;\n                  rdfs:label      ?part_label .\n            ?restriction a owl:Restriction ;\n                         owl:onProperty     ?relation ;\n                         owl:someValuesFrom obo:FMA_35175 .\n            OPTIONAL { ?kg_structure skos:exactMatch ?part }\n          }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_FMA_VM'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK'))\nORDER  BY CASE WHEN kg_structure IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END, part_label;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 10. What the FMA adds (3): case data grouped by anatomy\n\n**Question:** How many diagnoses concern a ligament of the knee joint – according to the FMA, not to a list we maintain?\n\n**How the query answers it:** Each injury in the knee graph carries the diagnosis code of the case data (`kg:caseDiagnosisCode`, section 7) and the structure it affects (`kg:affects`), which maps to an FMA class. Two `OPTIONAL` blocks ask the FMA: Is the class below *Ligament of knee joint* (FMA 44580, is-a)? Is it a part of the *Knee joint* (FMA 35175, part-of)? The result of `SEM_MATCH` is joined with the table `DIAGNOSES`; `GROUPING SETS` adds a subtotal per FMA group.\n\n**Result:** the FMA puts the anterior cruciate ligament and the tibial collateral ligament into the group *Ligament of knee joint* – together the cruciate ligament ruptures (80) and the medial ligament strains (61) from section 7. The sprain affects the joint capsule: part of the knee joint, but not a ligament. Patella and ankle joint are neither. For the tibial collateral ligament the FMA lists its anterior and posterior part as parts of the knee joint, not the ligament as a whole – so the part-of column stays empty there.\n\n**What the FMA adds:** aggregation on a standard anatomical level. A new diagnosis is grouped correctly as soon as its structure is mapped to the FMA; nobody maintains the groups by hand."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT NVL(k.fma_group, '(not a ligament of the knee joint)')      AS fma_is_a,\n       CASE WHEN GROUPING(k.structure) = 1 THEN '= subtotal' ELSE k.structure END AS structure,\n       k.part_of                                                        AS fma_part_of,\n       k.diagnosis_code,\n       COUNT(d.diagnosis_id)                                            AS diagnoses,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN d.confirmed_by = 'MRT' THEN 1 END)               AS confirmed_by_mri\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n          PREFIX obo:  <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/>\n          PREFIX owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#>\n          PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n          SELECT ?diagnosis_code ?structure ?fma_label ?fma_group ?part_of\n          WHERE {\n            ?injury kg:caseDiagnosisCode ?diagnosis_code ;\n                    kg:affects           ?s .\n            ?s rdfs:label      ?structure ;\n               skos:exactMatch ?fma .\n            FILTER (lang(?structure) = \"en\")\n            ?fma rdfs:label ?fma_label .\n            OPTIONAL { ?fma rdfs:subClassOf+ obo:FMA_44580 .\n                       obo:FMA_44580 rdfs:label ?fma_group }\n            OPTIONAL { ?fma rdfs:subClassOf ?r .\n                       ?r owl:someValuesFrom obo:FMA_35175 .\n                       obo:FMA_35175 rdfs:label ?part_of }\n          }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_FMA_VM'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK')) k\nLEFT   JOIN diagnoses d ON d.diagnosis_code = k.diagnosis_code\nGROUP  BY GROUPING SETS ((k.fma_group, k.structure, k.part_of, k.diagnosis_code), (k.fma_group))\nORDER  BY k.fma_group NULLS LAST, GROUPING(k.structure), diagnoses DESC;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 11. Inferred triples (1): what does the reasoner add?\n\nSection 4 used the inferred triples; here we look at them directly.\n\n**Question:** Which triples exist only because of the entailment `KNEE_KG_RIX` (OWLPRIME + rulebase `KNEE_RB`)?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The same SPARQL query counts the triples per predicate twice: once on `KNEE_KG` alone (asserted triples), once with `SEM_RULEBASES('OWLPRIME', 'KNEE_RB')` (asserted plus inferred). A `LEFT JOIN` on the predicate keeps the predicates that gain triples.\n\n**Result:** 9 inferred triples in 4 rows. OWLPRIME closes the class hierarchy: `rdfs:subClassOf` 6 → 8. `KNEE_RB` adds the conclusions about Jonas' report: `kg:matchesMechanismOf` 0 → 3, `kg:showsCounterIndicatorOf` 0 → 2, `kg:showsIndicatorOf` 0 → 2. The entailment is small because it covers only `KNEE_KG`, not the FMA (see `09a_load_knee_kg.sql`)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT w.p                                       AS predicate,\n       NVL(TO_NUMBER(a.n), 0)                    AS asserted,\n       TO_NUMBER(w.n)                            AS with_inference,\n       TO_NUMBER(w.n) - NVL(TO_NUMBER(a.n), 0)   AS inferred\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'SELECT ?p (COUNT(*) AS ?n) WHERE { ?s ?p ?o } GROUP BY ?p',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), SEM_RULEBASES('OWLPRIME', 'KNEE_RB'), NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK')) w\nLEFT   JOIN TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'SELECT ?p (COUNT(*) AS ?n) WHERE { ?s ?p ?o } GROUP BY ?p',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK')) a\n       ON a.p = w.p\nWHERE  TO_NUMBER(w.n) > NVL(TO_NUMBER(a.n), 0)\nORDER  BY predicate;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 12. Inferred triples (2): OWLPRIME at work\n\n**Question:** Which structures are a *ligament of knee joint* – with and without inference?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The knee graph states only direct subclasses: the anterior cruciate ligament is a cruciate ligament, the cruciate ligament is a ligament of the knee joint. The query asks for the subclasses of `kg:LigamentOfKneeJoint` twice – without and with `SEM_RULEBASES` – and marks the rows that only the inference returns.\n\nThis is the fourth rule on slide 13: **IF** A is a kind of B **AND** B is a kind of C **THEN** A is a kind of C.\n\n**Result:** 4 ligaments without inference (cruciate, fibular collateral, patellar, tibial collateral), 6 with inference: the anterior and the posterior cruciate ligament are added through *cruciate ligament*. A query asks for the general class and also gets the specific ones, without listing them."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT w.ligament,\n       CASE WHEN a.ligament IS NULL THEN 'inferred (OWLPRIME)' ELSE 'asserted' END AS source\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n          PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          SELECT ?ligament\n          WHERE {\n            ?c rdfs:subClassOf kg:LigamentOfKneeJoint .\n            ?c rdfs:label      ?ligament .\n            FILTER (lang(?ligament) = \"en\")\n          }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), SEM_RULEBASES('OWLPRIME', 'KNEE_RB'), NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK')) w\nLEFT   JOIN TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n          PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          SELECT ?ligament\n          WHERE {\n            ?c rdfs:subClassOf kg:LigamentOfKneeJoint .\n            ?c rdfs:label      ?ligament .\n            FILTER (lang(?ligament) = \"en\")\n          }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK')) a\n       ON a.ligament = w.ligament\nORDER  BY source, w.ligament;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 13. Inferred triples and the FMA in one query\n\n**Question:** Which pattern does Jonas' report fit – indicator and matching mechanism, no counter-indicator – and where in the reference anatomy is the structure it affects?\n\n**How the query answers it:** On the graph collection `KNEE_FMA_VM` the inferred triples (`kg:showsIndicatorOf`, `kg:matchesMechanismOf`, `kg:showsCounterIndicatorOf`) are ordinary triples next to `KNEE_KG` and the FMA. `FILTER NOT EXISTS` drops patterns with a counter-indicator. The path continues with `kg:suggests/kg:affects` and `skos:exactMatch` into the FMA and its part-of restriction.\n\nThe FMA part is the fifth rule on slide 13: it is not stored as inferred triples but followed at query time through `skos:exactMatch` and the part-of restriction of the FMA class.\n\n**Result:** one row: *Muster vordere Kreuzbandruptur* → anterior cruciate ligament → FMA 44614 *Anterior cruciate ligament*, part of the *Knee joint*. Run against `KNEE_KG` and `FMA` without the entailment, the same query returns no rows: the conclusion \"shows indicator of\" exists only as inferred triples."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT pattern_label,\n       structure,\n       fma_id,\n       fma_label,\n       part_of\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:       <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n          PREFIX owl:      <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#>\n          PREFIX rdfs:     <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          PREFIX skos:     <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n          PREFIX oboInOwl: <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#>\n          SELECT ?pattern_label ?structure ?fma_id ?fma_label ?part_of\n          WHERE {\n            <https://doag2026.example/knee#Report_SR-2026-11-16-0001> kg:showsIndicatorOf   ?pattern ;\n                                                                      kg:matchesMechanismOf ?pattern .\n            FILTER NOT EXISTS { <https://doag2026.example/knee#Report_SR-2026-11-16-0001> kg:showsCounterIndicatorOf ?pattern }\n            ?pattern rdfs:label ?pattern_label .\n            FILTER (lang(?pattern_label) = \"de\")\n            ?pattern kg:suggests/kg:affects ?s .\n            ?s rdfs:label ?structure .\n            FILTER (lang(?structure) = \"en\")\n            ?s skos:exactMatch ?fma .\n            ?fma rdfs:label  ?fma_label ;\n                 oboInOwl:id ?fma_id .\n            OPTIONAL { ?fma rdfs:subClassOf ?r .\n                       ?r a owl:Restriction ;\n                          owl:someValuesFrom ?whole .\n                       ?whole rdfs:label ?part_of }\n          }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_FMA_VM'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK'));"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 14. From the inferred pattern to diagnosis, next step and medication\n\nThis is the bottom row of slide 13 as one query: the conclusions of the rules decide the pattern, the knowledge graph and the FMA explain it, the case data add the medication.\n\n**Question:** Which of the patterns whose mechanism fits Jonas' report survives the rules – and what follows from it: diagnosis, structure in the FMA, next step and the medication given in earlier cases with that diagnosis?\n\n**How the query answers it:** `SEM_MATCH` on the graph collection `KNEE_FMA_VM` (it contains the entailment `KNEE_KG_RIX`) starts from the inferred triple `kg:matchesMechanismOf` and looks up with `OPTIONAL` whether the report also shows an indicator or a counter-indicator of the pattern. The `CASE` expression turns that into a verdict. The same pattern returns the injury it suggests with its diagnosis code (`kg:caseDiagnosisCode`), the recommended procedure with its urgency (`kg:recommends`, `kg:urgencyHours`) and, through `skos:exactMatch`, the FMA class of the affected structure and the whole it is part of. The result is joined with `DIAGNOSES` and `MEDICATION_RECOMMENDATIONS` on the diagnosis code; `COUNT(DISTINCT …)` gives the number of cases with each medication next to all cases with that diagnosis.\n\n**Result:** 7 rows for 3 patterns.\n- *passt* – ACL tear pattern → *Vordere Kreuzbandruptur* → FMA:44614 *Anterior cruciate ligament*, part of the *Knee joint* → *MRT innerhalb von 48 h*. Medication in the 80 earlier ACL rupture cases: ibuprofen 600 mg (76), enoxaparin 40 mg (45, thrombosis prophylaxis while the leg is immobilised), pantoprazole 20 mg (25).\n- *ausgeschlossen: Gegenindiz* – sprain pattern (mechanism and an indicator fit, but counter-indicators rule it out) → *Kniedistorsion*: ibuprofen 400 mg (76 of 115), diclofenac gel (75) – the \"rest and cool\" of the similarity search in 11c.\n- *ausgeschlossen: kein Indiz* – meniscus pattern (only the mechanism fits) → *Innenmeniskusläsion*: ibuprofen 400 mg (36 of 61), diclofenac gel (26).\n\nWithout the entailment the query returns no rows: every row starts from an inferred triple. Synthetic data, no therapy advice."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH kg AS (\n  SELECT CASE WHEN ctr IS NOT NULL THEN 'ausgeschlossen: Gegenindiz'\n              WHEN ind IS NULL     THEN 'ausgeschlossen: kein Indiz'\n              ELSE 'passt' END                                           AS verdict,\n         pattern_label,\n         injury_label,\n         diagnosis_code,\n         fma_id || ' ' || fma_label\n           || NVL2(whole_label, ' · Teil von ' || whole_label, '')       AS fma_structure,\n         procedure_label || ' innerhalb von ' || hours || ' h'           AS next_step\n  FROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n           'PREFIX kg:       <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n            PREFIX owl:      <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#>\n            PREFIX rdfs:     <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n            PREFIX skos:     <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n            PREFIX oboInOwl: <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#>\n            SELECT ?pattern_label ?ind ?ctr ?diagnosis_code ?injury_label\n                   ?fma_id ?fma_label ?whole_label ?procedure_label ?hours\n            WHERE {\n              <https://doag2026.example/knee#Report_SR-2026-11-16-0001> kg:matchesMechanismOf ?pattern .\n              OPTIONAL { <https://doag2026.example/knee#Report_SR-2026-11-16-0001> ?ind ?pattern .\n                         FILTER (?ind = kg:showsIndicatorOf) }\n              OPTIONAL { <https://doag2026.example/knee#Report_SR-2026-11-16-0001> ?ctr ?pattern .\n                         FILTER (?ctr = kg:showsCounterIndicatorOf) }\n              ?pattern rdfs:label      ?pattern_label ;\n                       kg:suggests     ?injury ;\n                       kg:recommends   ?procedure ;\n                       kg:urgencyHours ?hours .\n              ?injury  rdfs:label           ?injury_label ;\n                       kg:caseDiagnosisCode ?diagnosis_code ;\n                       kg:affects           ?structure .\n              ?procedure rdfs:label ?procedure_label .\n              FILTER (lang(?pattern_label)   = \"de\")\n              FILTER (lang(?injury_label)    = \"de\")\n              FILTER (lang(?procedure_label) = \"de\")\n              ?structure skos:exactMatch ?fma .\n              ?fma rdfs:label  ?fma_label ;\n                   oboInOwl:id ?fma_id .\n              OPTIONAL { ?fma rdfs:subClassOf ?r .\n                         ?r a owl:Restriction ;\n                            owl:someValuesFrom ?whole .\n                         ?whole rdfs:label ?whole_label }\n            }',\n           SEM_MODELS('KNEE_FMA_VM'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n           'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK'))),\ncases AS (\n  SELECT diagnosis_code, COUNT(*) AS diagnoses\n  FROM   diagnoses\n  GROUP  BY diagnosis_code)\nSELECT kg.verdict,\n       kg.pattern_label,\n       kg.injury_label                    AS diagnosis,\n       kg.fma_structure,\n       kg.next_step,\n       m.medication_text                  AS medication,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT d.diagnosis_id)     AS cases_with_medication,\n       c.diagnoses                        AS cases_with_diagnosis\nFROM   kg\nJOIN   cases c                        ON c.diagnosis_code = kg.diagnosis_code\nJOIN   diagnoses d                    ON d.diagnosis_code = kg.diagnosis_code\nJOIN   medication_recommendations m   ON m.diagnosis_id   = d.diagnosis_id\nGROUP  BY kg.verdict, kg.pattern_label, kg.injury_label, kg.fma_structure, kg.next_step,\n          m.medication_text, c.diagnoses\nORDER  BY CASE kg.verdict WHEN 'passt' THEN 0 ELSE 1 END, kg.pattern_label, cases_with_medication DESC;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Summary\n\n- The vector search found similar cases, the knowledge graph checks them against rules: Jonas' findings fit the **ACL rupture** pattern (3 of 3 strong indicators), not the sprain pattern (3 counter-indicators).\n- The answer comes with its reason: findings → pattern → anterior cruciate ligament (FMA 44614) → **MRI within 48 hours**.\n- **What the FMA adds:** the classification (twelve levels above the anterior cruciate ligament), the parts of the knee joint (20, of which the rules cover 7) and a grouping of the case data by standard anatomy – without modelling any of it ourselves.\n- **Inferred triples:** 9 triples from OWLPRIME and `KNEE_RB`; a query sees them with `SEM_RULEBASES` or on `KNEE_FMA_VM`, and they combine with the FMA in one query.\n- **From the conclusion to the action (slide 13):** the rules leave the ACL tear pattern; the graph and the FMA add the diagnosis (anterior cruciate ligament, part of the knee joint) and the next step (MRI within 48 hours), the case data the medication of earlier ACL ruptures – ibuprofen 600 mg and enoxaparin instead of the sprain's diclofenac gel (section 14).\n- Next: **11f** – who can do the MRI in time, and where?"
    languageId: markdown
